   "": {
    "actClause": "Concurrent Resolution of the Senate and Assembly proposing an amendment to\nsections 11 and 16 of article 6 of the constitution, in relation to the civil\njurisdiction of county and district courts",
    "basePrintNo": "S9731",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9731-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd Art 6 §§11 & 16, Constn",
    "lawSection": "Constitution, Concurrent Resolutions to Amend",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9731\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN-SIGAL\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nCONCURRENT RESOLUTION OF THE SENATE AND ASSEMBLY proposing an amendment\nto sections 11 and 16 of article 6 of the constitution, in relation to\nthe civil jurisdiction of county and district courts\n\n \nSOURCE OF BILL:\n\nThis concurrent resolution is being introduced at the request of Judici-\nary.\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL::\n\nThis concurrent resolution would amend Article VI of the State Constitu-\ntion to recognize the effects of inflation by increasing the maximum\ncivil monetary jurisdiction of the County Courts from $25,000 to\n$50,000, and of the District Courts from $15,000 to $35,000.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS::\n\nSection 1 of the resolution amends section 11(a) of Article VI to\nincrease the maximum civil monetary jurisdiction of the County Courts\nfrom $25,000 to $50,000.\n\nSection 2 of the resolution amends section 16(d) of Article VI to\nincrease the maximum civil monetary jurisdiction of the District Courts\nfrom $15,000 to $35,000.\n\nSection 3 of the resolution provides that, once approved by the Legisla-\nture, the amendments to sections 11(a) and 16(d) must be referred to the\nlegislative session convening after the next general election of members\nof the Legislature.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION::\n\nAt present, sections 11(a) and 16(d) of Article VI fix the maximum civil\nmonetary jurisdiction of the County Courts and District Courts at\n$25,000 and $15,000, respectively.\n\nThese caps were fixed in 1983, replacing the $6,000 cap that had then\nbeen in effect for both courts since 1962. The 1983 changes were to\nadjust for inflation over the preceding two decades.\n\nToday, more than 40 years have elapsed since the $25,000 and $15,000\ncaps were fixed. During that time, inflation has decreased the value of\nthe dollar by over 200%.1 This concurrent resolution proposes a 100%\nincrease in the County Court jurisdictional limit (from $25,000 to $50,\n000) and a 133% increase in the District Court jurisdictional limit\n(from $15,000 to $35,000). Both increases seek to strike a balance\nbetween adjusting for inflation and making sure that the County and\nDistrict courts remain able to handle the increased workload that will\nresult from increasing the jurisdictional limit.  In addition, the\nchange will ensure that the County Courts and the District Courts are\nable to handle a meaningful amount of the civil litigation, thereby\nrelieving Supreme Court of some of the caseload burden that it now must\nbear.\nThe increases called for by this concurrent resolution are consistent\nwith the 100% increase (from $25,000 to $50,000) in the civil jurisdic-\ntion of the New York City Civil Court adopted by the voters in November\n2021 and effective January 1, 2022. In that Court, the jurisdictional\ncap had last been fixed in 1983, and the 2020 and 2021 Legislatures\nbelieved it necessary to increase it to adjust for four decades' worth\nof inflation.\n\nIf approved by the Legislature in 2024, this concurrent resolution will\nthen be submitted for second passage in 2025 and, if again approved by\nthe Legislature, the constitutional amendments for which it provides\nwill then be submitted to the State's voters for their approval. See NY\nConst., Art. XIX, § 1. If approved by the voters, these amendments would\ntake effect on January first next thereafter.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY::\n\nNone. This is a new proposal.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS::\n\nThis concurrent resolution would have no State fiscal impact. 8.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE::\n\nThe amendments proposed in this concurrent resolution would take effect\non January Pt next following ratification by the voters.\n\n(1) See CPI Inflation Calculator:\nhttps://www.officialdata.orgius/intlation/.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
